<DevelopmentPlan VERSION="1.0.0">
  <ArchitectureNotes>
    <objective>One append-only, versioned Entity hypertable plus a DB-driven Schema registry: every write inserts a new version, graph edges live as embedded jsonb links, and PostgreSQL/TimescaleDB triggers enforce validation, versioning, cascade-delete and change-notify so the TypeScript client stays a thin query/plan compiler.</objective>
    <non-goal>Not a code-first ORM: class definitions live in the DB Schema table (seeded/synced), not as TS classes; there are no code migrations. No arbitrary-SQL builder — only the chain DSL and the fixed auth/authz table APIs.</non-goal>
    <risk-1>Type-level import cycle M-SQL &#8596; M-ACL &#8596; M-TABLES: sql imports aclDenied (value) from acl; acl imports Tables (type) from tables; tables imports Ctx (type) from sql. Only the sql&#8594;acl edge is a runtime value import, so there is no runtime cycle, but the coupling constrains refactors.</risk-1>
    <risk-2>Runtime ESM cycle M-CONNECT &#8596; M-UP: index re-exports up, up imports connect. Safe today via ESM live bindings (no top-level use), but fragile.</risk-2>
    <risk-3>Logic concentration: chain.ts, sql.ts and write.ts hold most of the engine (each an order of magnitude larger than the other modules); large surfaces for regressions. Exact sizes intentionally not quoted here — they drift; measure the files.</risk-3>
    <risk-4>Invariant duplication: entity validation and link-end rules exist both in M-DDL triggers (entity_check) and in M-WRITE.validate — they must stay in sync.</risk-4>
  </ArchitectureNotes>

